### MEDIUM: gemini (gemini-code-assist[bot])
**File**: `script.js:201`
![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Using `setTimeout` with a fixed delay to scroll to a hash target on page load can be unreliable. It creates a race condition where the scroll might execute before the page has fully rendered, especially on slower connections or complex pages, leading to incorrect scroll positioning.

A more robust solution is to use the `window.load` event, which ensures all page resources (including stylesheets that affect layout) are loaded before attempting to scroll.

```javascript
    window.addEventListener('load', () => {
        if (window.location.hash) {
            const hashTarget = document.querySelector(window.location.hash);
            if (hashTarget) {
                hashTarget.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
            }
        }
    });
```

